<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Banovina of Croatia</h1><p class="st-meta">1939 CE – 1941 CE · capital Zagreb</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Autonomous Croatian province created in 1939 in a late attempt to settle the Serb-Croat conflict within the Kingdom of Yugoslavia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-yugoslavia">Kingdom of Yugoslavia</a> had been divided in 1929 into banovinas that ignored historic borders. With war looming, the regent Prince Paul sought agreement with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatian-peasant-party">Croatian Peasant Party</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cvetkovic-macek-agreement">Cvetković-Maček Agreement</a> of 26 August 1939 merged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sava">Sava</a> and Littoral banovinas with parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herzegovina">Herzegovina</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syrmia">Srem</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubrovnik">Dubrovnik</a> area into one Banovina of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The banovina had autonomy over internal administration, education, justice and the economy under a ban, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-subasic">Ivan Šubašić</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladko-macek">Vladko Maček</a> became deputy prime minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatian-parliament">Croatian Sabor</a> was planned but never elected. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> in the banovina feared Croatian domination, while the separatist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ustase">Ustaše</a> rejected any settlement within Yugoslavia.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> invasion of April 1941, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ustase">Ustaše</a> proclaimed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/independent-state-of-croatia">Independent State of Croatia</a> on 10 April, ending the banovina. Its borders influenced later debates about <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia's</a> territory, including the partition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> discussed in the 1990s.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/banovina-of-croatia.jpg" alt="Banovina of Croatia" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Ex13 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Banate_of_Croatia_(1939-1941).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1939 CE – 1941 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Zagreb</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ivan Šubašić, Vladko Maček</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic and Orthodox Christianity, Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Croatian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/croatia">Croatia</a>, <a href="/countries/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Banate of Croatia (1939)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1940&amp;focus=banovina-of-croatia" aria-label="Banovina of Croatia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5058 563 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Banovina of Croatia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5378 819L5488 850M5378 819L5378 819"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5488 850h0M5378 819h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5378 819h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5378 819h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Zagreb. <a href="/atlas?year=1940&amp;focus=banovina-of-croatia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
